<p>You don't need a customer for every sale: most go through as <strong>Walk-in</strong>. Add the people you want to know again:
  contractors, fundis, regulars, and anyone who buys on account or needs a tax invoice. Customers live in
  <strong>Customers › Customers</strong>.</p>

<h2>Adding a customer</h2>
<ol>
  <li>On <strong>Customers</strong>, press <strong>New</strong>.</li>
  <li>Type their <strong>Name</strong> and <strong>Phone</strong>. The phone number finds them at the till and is where texted receipts go.</li>
  <li>Owners and managers can set a <strong>Credit limit</strong> in shillings. 0 means cash only; above 0 lets them buy on account.</li>
  <li>Under <strong>More options</strong>, add their <strong>Email</strong>, <strong>Tax PIN</strong> (their KRA PIN, for tax invoices) and
    <strong>Address</strong> (for deliveries and statements). Owners and managers also choose their <strong>Prices</strong> and how many days
    they <strong>Pay within</strong>. There's room for <strong>Notes</strong> too.</li>
  <li>Press <strong>Add customer</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p>To change any of it later, open the customer and press <strong>Edit</strong>. The customer's KRA PIN appears on their tax invoices,
  quotes and statements, and goes to KRA with the sale when eTIMS is connected.</p>

<h2>Bringing your customers across from a spreadsheet</h2>
<p>If you're moving to HardPoint from a debtors' book, Excel or another system, the owner can bring every customer in at once, with what
  each one owes today.</p>
<ol>
  <li>On <strong>Customers</strong>, open the <strong>⚙</strong> menu and choose <strong>Import customers</strong>.</li>
  <li>Download the <a href="/customer-import-template.csv" class="link">template</a> and fill it in, one customer a line. Only
    <code>name</code> is needed. <code>phone</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>kra_pin</code>, <code>address</code>, <code>credit_limit</code>,
    <code>payment_terms_days</code> and <code>notes</code> are there if you have them.</li>
  <li>Put what a customer owes you in <code>balance</code>, and the day the debt is from in <code>balance_date</code>, written like
    13/11/2026. Leave the date out and it's today.</li>
  <li>Save the spreadsheet as CSV, choose the file and press <strong>Check and import</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p>HardPoint checks the whole file first. If any line has a problem (an amount that isn't a number, a date it can't read, the same customer
  twice) nothing is imported, and it lists the problems by line so you can fix the file and upload it again.</p>
<p>The balance goes on the customer's account as an <strong>opening balance</strong>. It shows on their page, their statement and
  <strong>Who owes what</strong> like an unpaid sale, it's due after their payment terms counted from its date, and their payments settle it
  first. It isn't a sale: it doesn't appear in your takings, your VAT or KRA eTIMS.</p>
<ul>
  <li>A line whose phone number matches a customer you already have updates that customer. With no phone, the name is matched.</li>
  <li>A balance is only set on an account with no sales or payments on it yet, so importing the same file twice can't double anyone's debt.</li>
  <li>For a customer who is in credit with you, put a minus in front of the balance, like -2500. The credit comes off what they buy next,
    or you can refund it from their page.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Contractor and wholesale prices</h2>
<p>Make a price list, such as Contractor, in <strong>Settings › Price lists</strong>, and give products their price on it under
  <strong>Special prices</strong> on each product page (see <%= link_to "Products and the catalogue", help_article_path("products"), class: "link" %>).
  Then set the customer's <strong>Prices</strong> to that list. Customers left on <strong>Retail</strong> pay the normal price.</p>

<h2>Choosing a customer at the till</h2>
<ol>
  <li>At the top of the cart, press <strong>Change</strong> next to <strong>Customer</strong>.</li>
  <li>Type part of their name or phone number and pick them. The cart is repriced at once with their price list, shown as a badge by
    their name.</li>
  <li>To go back to a cash sale, press <strong>Walk-in customer</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p>If they're not there yet, press <strong>New customer</strong> under the search. The form opens with the name or phone number you
  searched for. Fill in the rest and press <strong>Add and use on this sale</strong>: you're back at the till with the new customer on
  the sale. <strong>Back to the till</strong> returns without adding them.</p>

<h2>The customer page</h2>
<p>Open a customer from the list (search by name or phone) to see:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Their <strong>Prices</strong>, what the customer <strong>Owes on account</strong> (and how much is overdue), <strong>Credit left</strong> and
    how many days they pay within.</li>
  <li><strong>Unpaid account sales</strong> with their due dates, and <strong>Payments on account</strong>.</li>
  <li>Open quotes and orders, open jobs, and their <strong>Recent purchases</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The buttons along the top are <strong>New quote</strong>, <strong>Statement</strong>, <strong>Record payment</strong> (when they owe
  something) and, with contractor jobs switched on in <strong>Settings › Features</strong>, <strong>Add a job</strong>. When SMS is set up
  and they owe money, <strong>Text a reminder</strong> sends their balance to their phone, once a day at most. A statement can also go by PDF, email or
  WhatsApp. A customer who asks not to be texted gets <strong>Don't text this customer</strong> ticked on their record; after that no receipt,
  order text or reminder goes to their number.</p>

<%= render "help/walkthrough", tour: HelpTour.find("customer") %>

<h2>Who owes us</h2>
<p><strong>Customers › Who owes us</strong> lists every customer with a balance, split into not yet due, 1–30, 31–60, 61–90 and over 90
  days late, with a <strong>Statement</strong> link for each. Owners, managers and accountants can see it.</p>

